    "summary": "Liban Obsiye, Executive Director of Somalia's National Climate Change Fund, participated in a three-day regional conference in Salvador, Brazil, focusing on enhancing climate finance access for African and Latin American countries.",
    "content": "Liban Obsiye, the Executive Director of Somalia's National Climate Change Fund, represented the country at a three-day regional conference held in Salvador, Brazil, from September 10 to 12, 2026. The summit brought together financial officials and experts from African and Latin American nations to discuss strategies for improving access to and strengthening climate finance. The event specifically addressed the challenges that developing countries face in securing funding for climate adaptation and mitigation projects.\n\nThe conference took place against the backdrop of increasing global concern over climate change impacts, particularly in vulnerable regions such as the Horn of Africa. Somalia has experienced recurring droughts and floods in recent years, which have displaced communities and strained the country's limited resources. These events have underscored the need for robust international financial support to help Somalia build resilience and implement effective climate action plans. The National Climate Change Fund, established to coordinate and channel climate-related investments, plays a central role in these efforts.\n\nAt the summit, participants shared best practices and explored mechanisms to simplify and expedite access to climate funds. Discussions also focused on innovative financing models, including public-private partnerships and blended finance, to bridge the gap between available resources and the scale of need. For Somalia, which is both a contributor to and a victim of global climate challenges, such forums provide an opportunity to learn from other nations' experiences and to advocate for more equitable distribution of climate finance.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development matters significantly. Many diaspora members have family and business interests in Somalia and are directly affected by climate-induced disasters. They also contribute remittances that often serve as a lifeline during emergencies. Improved climate finance could lead to more sustainable development projects, reducing the need for repeated humanitarian appeals and enabling long-term investments in infrastructure, agriculture, and water management.\n\nAdditionally, the diaspora can play a role in supporting climate initiatives by facilitating knowledge transfer, investing in green projects, and advocating for Somalia's interests on international platforms. As climate change continues to threaten livelihoods and stability in Somalia, the outcomes of conferences like this one in Brazil will be closely watched by those who remain connected to the homeland.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/12/abaaraha-iyo-fatahaadaha-soomaaliya-oo-miiska-caalamka-la-saaray/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=abaaraha-iyo-fatahaadaha-soomaaliya-oo-miiska-caalamka-la-saaray",
